    "workedSolution": "**(a)** $3 \\times 2 - 1 = 5$.\n\n**(b)** $3 \\times 32 - 1 = 95$.\n\n**(c)** $3 \\times 2^n - 1 > 1000 \\Rightarrow 2^n > 333.7$ **(M1)**; $2^8 = 256$, $2^9 = 512$, so $n = 9$ **(A1)**.\n\n**(d)** Terms: $5, 11, 23, 47, 95$; each is double the previous plus 1.",
